[interviewer · INTRO] Your client is Brew & Bean, a specialty coffee chain with 200 locations across the US. Over the past two years, their net profit margin has declined from 24% to 6%, even though revenues have grown 15% over the same period. The CEO has hired us to identify the root cause of this profit decline and recommend how to reverse it. How would you approach this problem?

Take a minute if you need it, then tell me how you'd structure your approach.

[candidate silent 78s]

[interviewer · check_in · 1:01] Still with me? Take your time. The question on the table: how would you approach this problem?

[candidate · 2:48] Since revenue is actually up 15% while margin collapsed from 24% to 6%, the decline has to be driven by costs growing faster than revenue, so I'd weight my structure there — but I want to confirm the revenue side first so we don't miss a mix issue.

Three buckets:

First, revenue quality. Is the 15% growth coming from new store openings, same-store traffic, or price? Growth from new stores can carry heavy upfront costs and drag margin even if each store is healthy.

Second, costs — split fixed versus variable. Variable: coffee beans, dairy, cups, hourly labor. Fixed: rent, salaried staff, corporate overhead, marketing, any D&A from the expansion.

Third, external: commodity prices, wage legislation, competitive pricing pressure.

I'd start by asking which cost lines grew fastest in absolute dollars over the two years. Do we have a cost breakdown as a percent of revenue for both years?
