[interviewer · check_in · 6:45] Still with me? Take your time.

[candidate · 8:32] Let me size it. Two years ago COGS was 42% of revenue: beans at 25% of that is 10.5 points, other inputs 31.5 points.

If that 37.5% increase is per-unit, other inputs go to 31.5 × 1.375 = 43.3 points of revenue. Today's COGS is 58, so beans are 58 − 43.3 = 14.7 points, up from 10.5 — a 40% increase. So beans and everything else rose at roughly the same rate. This isn't a coffee-bean story, it's broad input inflation across the whole basket.

And the critical fact is menu prices haven't moved in two years. Revenue grew 15% with flat prices, so that's volume — traffic is healthy. Brew & Bean absorbed 100% of input inflation instead of passing any of it through.

One check: is that 37.5% figure per unit, or total dollars? If it's total dollars, 15% of it is just volume growth and the real inflation is closer to 20%.
